 { In this section we'll take a look at the battle for controlling an outpost. The topics we'll take a look at are: eliminating key pieces; prophylaxis; and fighting against outposts. } ( { This position was reached in the game Geller, E - Etcheverry, E (1954). White has an outpost on f5, but the immediate jump with } 16. Nf5 $2 { would be met by } Bxf5 { . So White sets about exchanging off Black's key piece. Can you guess what move he played? } ) 
16. { -KEY- } Bh3 $1 { Correct! Once the bishops come off the board, White's knight will be unassailable on f5. } Qf8 ( 16... Bb7 $2 { Black cannot keep the bishops on the board, since after } 17. Bf5 Kh8 18. Bxf6 { if Black recaptures with } Bxf6 { then } 19. Qh6 { is mate. } ) 
17. Rdg1 { White could have traded on c8, but there is no rush. } Bxh3 { Black lacks good options, so he himself initiates the exchange. } 18. Rxh3 Ncd7 19. Nf5 $1 { Now the knight arrives. One of the challenges facing Black is that neither of his knights are well-positioned to challenge White's outposted piece. } Bh8 20. Rhg3 { Building up the pressure on the open file. Black's position is hopeless. The rest of the game is included, with only light annotations. } Rg6 21. Nd1 { Preparing to bring the remaining minor piece to the kingside. } Ng8 22. Qe2 Ndf6 23. Nf2 Bg7 24. Nh3 Re8 25. Bd2 { Vacating the g5 square for the knight. } Ne7 26. Ng5+ Kh8 27. Qf2 Rc8 28. Ne6 ( { Black resigned, since } 28. Ne6 Rxg3 { loses the queen while } fxe6 29. Rxg6 Nxg6 30. dxe6 $1 { also leads to material losses. The key to White's strategy was the forced exchange of the light-squared bishops. Once he landed a monster knight on f5, it became impossible to defend the kingside. } ) 

