 ( { Here is another typical situation. White would like to play } 1. Nd5 { , but for now that square is controlled by Black's knight. } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Nfg4 $1 { A strong move! White forces Black's hand, securing the exchange of Black's key defensive piece. } Nxg4 { White is now facing a choice: to capture the knight with the queen or with the knight. From a positional standpoint, White wants to play Qxg4 so that their knight is ready to jump to the d5 outpost on the next move. However, Qxg4 allows Black to win a pawn with ...Rxd3. In cases like these, where the desirable positional move seems to have a tactical problem, it's worth spending some time to calculate the consequences fully. } ( 1... Ngh7 $2 { It's not possible for Black to try and hold the piece configuration, since the h-pawn is undefended. } 2. Nxh6 Kg7 3. Nhg4 $18 { White is a clean pawn to the good. } ) 
2. Qxg4 $1 { Capturing with the queen is best, leaving the knight on e3 in order to jump to the d5 outpost as soon as possible. } Rxd3 { Black grabs a pawn. White, however, had correctly calculated the consequences of this sacrifice. } ( 2... h5 { Anything other than grabbing the d3 pawn will lead to a risk-free edge for White. For example: } 3. Qe2 Rd7 4. Nd5 $16 { White's minor pieces are much stronger than Black's. } ) 
3. Nd5 $1 Qd8 ( 3... Rxd5 { Giving up the exchange is perhaps best. } 4. exd5 f5 5. Qe2 c4 6. Kh1 $16 { Black has some compensation here in the form of a healthy pawn chain and a little more space. But White has a clear edge overall since Black's advantages do not make up for being an exchange down. } ) 
4. h4 $1 { This is what White had seen. The attack on Black's knight means that White will recover their pawn, and have a crushing kingside attack to boot. } Ne6 ( 4... Nxe4 $2 { fails to } 5. Nxe7 $1 { followed by taking the knight on e4. } ) 
5. Bxh6 Re8 6. h5 $18 { White's attack crashes through. Targeting the defensive piece is usually quite straightforward, while other aspects of the fight for outposts are easier to overlook. One such method is 'prophylaxis', and will be covered in the next couple of examples. } 
