 { In this position, White failed to sense the danger and castled queenside. } 1. { -KEY- } O-O-O $2 ( 1. Qe2 $1 { Prophylaxis! By protecting the f3 square White would have kept a big advantage, since now Black's queen and knight cannot move. } ) 
Nf3 $1 { Now Black can use the weak f3 square to target the central outpost. } 2. Qe2 Nd4 $1 { Black lands their knight on the powerful d4 outpost. White now faces a difficult choice between retreating with the queen or winning a pawn by capturing the knight. } 3. Bxd4 { White goes after the pawn, but this is dangerous since now Black's dark-squared bishop will become very active. } exd4 4. Nb5 d3 $1 { The best way of giving up the pawn, since this way White cannot play 5.Nxd4. And as we'll soon see, the knight on b5 is not so well-placed. } 5. Bxd3 Bd7 $17 { Black stands better due to their much more active pieces. } 6. Bc2 ( 6. Nxc7 $4 { loses to } Rac8 { since } 7. Nb5 Bxb5 $19 8. cxb5 Nxd3 { wins a piece. } ) 
a4 7. a3 Ra5 8. Kb1 Bxb5 9. cxb5 Qf6 { White has no good way to meet the pressure against b2. For example: } 10. Bd3 Nxd3 11. Rxd3 Rxb5 { We see the point of 7...Ra5 earlier! The pressure along the dark squares is fatal for White, a direct consequence of allowing the ...Nf3-d4 maneuver in the beginning. } 
