 { Can you find the only move that holds the balance for Black here? To arrive at the answer, it is helpful to ask yourself what White's main positional threat is. } 1... { -KEY- } a5 $1 { Correct! By playing this move, Black takes the sting out of White's positional idea. } ( 1... Bd7 $2 { If Black fails to sense the positional danger, then White will be able to shut out Black's knight. } 2. a3 $1 Na6 3. b4 $16 { This is the 'trap' that Black must avoid. Black's knight will struggle to find activity in the middlegame since the b4 and c5 squares have been taken away. } ) 
2. a3 Na6 3. b4 { Playing their idea anyway! The problem for White is that now the b4 pawn is not stable. } ( 3. Nf1 { If White had continued quietly, then Black would have locked in the outpost on c5 by fixing White's queenside pawn structure. } Nc5 4. Ng3 a4 $1 { Note the link between outposts and backward pawns. Here Black has fixed White's b-pawn as a backward pawn by controlling the b3 square, and this in turn secures the c5 square as an outpost. } ) 
Bd7 { With the rook defended, Black is now threatening to capture on b4. } ( 3... axb4 { Black cannot win a pawn on b4 because of the rook. } 4. axb4 Nxb4 $4 5. Rxa8 $18 ) 
4. Qb3 axb4 5. axb4 { The b4 pawn restricts the knight, but it is isolated and so it becomes an immediate target. } Qb6 $1 6. Ba3 { White has to play the bishop to the passive a3 square to avoid losing material. } Bb5 $11 { The bishop steps onto an active diagonal. Even though White succeeded in robbing Black of the c5 square, the position remains around equal. This is because White's b4 pawn is a bit weak and their piece coordination is poor. Neither of these factors would have been true if White had been allowed to play b2-b4 without the need to exchange a-pawns. For our final two examples, we'll switch perspective and speak about ways in which the defending side can fight against outposts. } 
