 { Previously, we looked at a couple of examples where the attacking side forced a key piece exchange in order to access the outpost. The defending side can use this same method to defuse an outpost. In the following position, we see that Black is threatening to land a knight on the c4 outpost. White stops this plan in its tracks. } 1. { -KEY- } Ndf3 $1 { Using the pin along the d-file to force one pair of knights off the board. } ( 1. a4 $2 { Things would go downhill fast if White had failed to spot the danger: } Nc4 $1 2. Qc3 { White has to pin their queen to avoid losing the b4 pawn. } Qg4 $1 { With a double attack on d4 and h4. } 3. Ngf3 { Much like in our previous example, White is being forced to go passive and react to Black's constant threats. } Rfe8 $1 4. Rfd1 Re4 5. b5 axb5 6. axb5 Nxe3 { The series of exchanges will expose White's fragile king. } 7. fxe3 Nxd4 8. Nxd4 Ra2 $1 9. Rd2 Rxd2 10. Qxd2 Bxd4 11. exd4 Re2 $19 { Chess can be an unforgiving game! The second that Black's knight hit the c4 outpost, it was close to impossible to save the position. } ) 
Rfe8 { This is the best move according to the engine, but now White can continue their plan of removing the knights from the board. } ( 1... Nc4 $4 { is a blunder. } 2. Rxc4 $1 { wins a piece, since } dxc4 { is not possible because of } 3. Qxd7 $18 ) 
 ( 1... Rad8 { Black can insist on driving the knight to c4. However, in this case White can take the sting out of the outpost by forcing a series of piece exchanges. } 2. Nxe5 Nxe5 3. Bd4 $1 { After exchanging one pair of knights, White prepares the exchange of bishops. } Nc4 4. Qc3 Bxd4 5. Qxd4 $14 { The reduced material on the board reduces the influence of Black's outposted knight, while making Black's weaker structure a more significant factor. } ) 
2. Nxe5 Nxe5 3. Bf4 $5 { Targeting the last remaining knight, taking advantage of the fact that ...Nc4 continues to be impossible because of Rxc4. } Rad8 4. Bxe5 $1 Rxe5 5. Nf3 $11 { Now that both of Black's knights have been eliminated, the c4 outpost is not significant. In this example we focused on two ideas. The first is to exchange the piece before it can reach the outpost. If this is not possible, then you can also try to seek out other piece exchanges to reduce the value of the outpost. In our final example, we'll look at more aggressive measures that can be taken when a piece has already established itself on the outpost. } 
