 { Sometimes, you will find yourself having lost the battle for the outposts. When you find yourself in a critical situation like this, you must avoid letting your opponent consolidate their advantage. The way to do this is by finding tactical opportunities and ways of generating counterplay. In the current position, Black's situation is critical since White has two monster knights on e5 and c5. In the absence of tactical opportunities, this type of positional advantage is enough to win. For this reason, Black must find a way to strike immediately. } 1... { -KEY- } Bxe5 $1 { The first step in Black's plan. By capturing on e5, White's knight on c5 is destabilised. It goes from sitting on an outpost to a weak square, and this adds potency to Black's next move. } ( 1... f4 $2 { Immediately challenging White's bishop plays into White's hands. For example: } 2. Bd2 Bxe5 3. Qxe5 $1 Nc6 4. Qxe6 $18 { White is two pawns to the good. } ) 
2. dxe5 f4 $1 { Black is playing concrete and aggressive chess, the right prescription for situations where you are under positional pressure. } 3. Bd4 { Since the knight is no longer supported by a pawn, White's reply is forced. } ( 3. Bd2 $4 Rxc5 $19 ) 
f3 $1 { The first positional gain! The pawn break fractures White's pawn chain. } 4. Qe4 $1 { White is suddenly the one forced to be very precise. Any other move besides this would have lost. } ( 4. gxf3 $4 { The pawn is poisoned. } Nf4 $1 { With the White bishop no longer patrolling the c1-h6 diagonal, Black's knight can jump into f4 with an attack on the queen. } 5. Qe4 Qg5 { Black's queen steps in with check. No matter what White plays, Black will reply with Qg2#. } ) 
 ( 4. Qd2 $2 { also loses after } Nf4 5. Rfe1 Qg5 6. g3 Nxh3 { Black wins the white queen. } ) 
fxg2 { The damaged structure has left weak squares on f3, f4 and h4 that Black can hope to target. } ( 4... Nf4 $2 { Now this idea no longer works, as White can reply with } 5. Qxf3 $18 ) 
5. Kxg2 { White is up a pawn, but Black is the one with the safer king. } ( 5. Qxg2 $2 Nf4 $1 6. Qg4 Nc6 7. Be3 { Bd4-c3 would lose the knight on c5, but now White's central pawn falls. } Nxe5 $19 { White is powerless against the combined threats on the kingside. } ) 
Nf4+ { A mating nets begin to form around the White king. } 6. Kh2 Nc6 { Threatening ...Nxd4 followed by ...Nf4-e2, winning material. } 7. Be3 Qh4 8. Bxf4 { White has to eliminate the strong piece. } Rxf4 9. Qg2 Rcf8 $1 { Targeting the f2 pawn. } ( 9... Nd4 { This move looks very strong, but runs into a crushing tactic. } 10. Nxe6 $1 { Suddenly White threatens checkmate both on g7 and along the back rank! } Rg8 11. Nxf4 $18 { White is two pawns and an exchange to the good. } ) 
10. Nd3 { Already for 9 moves White has been forced to react to Black's play. Finally, White has to retreat the advanced knight to tend to the threats against their king. } Rf3 11. Rxc6 Rxd3 { In spite of White's extra pawn (and the possibility of winning a second one on e6), the position is equal due to White's weakened structure and problems of king safety. Importantly for Black, the character of the game has changed completely. White's outposts on e5 and c5 no longer exist, and neither do the knights that previously occupied them! } 12. Rxe6 h6 13. Rg6 Qf4+ 14. Kh1 Qxe5 $11 { This brings us to the end of this section. In the fight for an outpost, well-timed piece exchanges, prophylactic moves, and an ability to shift from a positional to a dynamic battle at the right moment are all essential tools that will help you improve your results in this kind of confrontations at the chessboard. Staying alert with sharp prophylactic moves is also important. Finally, when all else fails as in this final position, then an all-out tactical war may be the only path left. After all, a positional bind is like your enemy putting you in a straitjacket. Once you sense their intentions, you must find a way to escape before your fate is sealed. Two sections remain in this chapter, in both cases introducing new terms relating to outposts. The first of these are called 'power outposts', while the second and final topic will cover 'pseudo outposts'. Let's dive in! } 
